{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And every meal-offering that is baked in the oven</b> The way of the plain sense is well known here, that Scripture commands concerning one who vows one of the three meal-offerings — oven-baked, the pan (marcheshet), and the griddle (machavat) — that it shall belong to the priest who offers it alone. And concerning all the other meal-offerings — for example, one who vows a meal-offering unspecified (stam), who brings fine flour (solet), and the meal-offering of first fruits, which are mixed with oil, and the meal-offering of a sinner (חוטא) and of a suspected woman (סוטה), which are barren of oil — it says they shall be divided among all the sons of Aaron, that is, among all their father's houses. And the meaning of \"and every meal-offering mixed with oil and dry\" is a meal-offering that has nothing but fine flour mixed or dry, not from those [three] mentioned above. And the reason for the distinction between them would be because the priest labored in their baking and it is fitting to multiply his reward.\n\nBut our Rabbis (Menahot 73a) did not accept this, because it says \"and every meal-offering mixed with oil and dry,\" which includes all the meal-offerings in the world, since they are all either mixed or dry. Therefore they held that the meaning of \"to the priest who offers it\" is — to the pure priests who are present there. And likewise what it says (Leviticus 7:8) \"and the priest who offers a man's burnt-offering\" and \"the priest who makes atonement with it, it shall be his\" (Leviticus 7:7) — all of these come only to say that they shall not belong to the owners, but they shall be, as wages for the offering, to the pure priests present there, for all of them are offering by hand or by instruction. For the single individual among them, or the two or three who officiate, act with the permission of all of them and as their agents, and all of them stand over the offering — as the portion that goes down to battle and the portion that stays by the baggage, together they divide. And after it said that they shall belong to the priests as wages for their service, it returned and clarified \"and every meal-offering mixed with oil and dry,\" which is the general rule for all meal-offerings, \"to all the sons of Aaron\" the officiating priests mentioned, \"it shall be, each man as his brother,\" that is, to the father's house of those among them who are pure, all of whom are the officiating priests mentioned. And Scripture mentioned the meal-offerings by their names — oven-baked, pan (marcheshet), and griddle (machavat) — and afterward returned and generalized, saying \"each man as his brother,\" so that this one shall have nothing but what that one has, and even the meal-offering of fine flour (סולת) they shall divide. And Scripture said that this rule applies to meal-offerings, and all the more so to the rest of the sacrifices, whose value is greater, and the received tradition (kabbalah) shall decide. And this is a regulation for the priests and the peace of the Temple. And it is possible that the structure of the verse follows this view: \"and every meal-offering that is baked in the oven\" and \"every one made in the pan (marcheshet)\" and \"on the griddle (machavat)\" — \"to the priest who offers it, it shall be,\" and \"every meal-offering mixed with oil and dry\" — likewise, and \"to all the sons of Aaron it shall be, each man as his brother.\""], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The references to Menahot 73a correspond to the Talmudic discussion in tractate Menahot. The phrase והקבלה תכריע (\"the received tradition shall decide\") refers to the halakhic tradition, not the kabbalistic sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:51:26.850540606Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}